        <p class="profile-detail-region">Life · Central &amp; East Asia</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Emperor Wu of Han</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">156 BCE – 87 BCE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Born</span><span class="info-stat-val">156 BCE, on the day his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-jing-of-han">Liu Qi</a> acceded as Emperor Jing of Han; son of Emperor Jing and Wang Zhi, of the Liu family of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Died</span><span class="info-stat-val">29 March 87 BCE, after a serious illness that began by 88 BCE; buried in the Maoling mound</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Who they were</span><span class="info-stat-val">Prince of Jiaodong from 153 BCE; crown prince from 149 BCE; seventh emperor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> from 141 BCE until his death in 87 BCE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">the longest reign of an ethnic Han emperor; wars against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a>; the missions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-qian">Zhang Qian</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-regions">Western Regions</a>; the annexation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanyue">Nanyue</a>, Dian and Gojoseon; the promotion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucianism">Confucianism</a> and the Imperial University; the Taichu calendar; the Imperial Music Bureau; harsh punishments and the witchcraft persecutions</span></div>
        </div>
