    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">156 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Liu Che is born on the day his father accedes as Emperor Jing</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">141 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">He succeeds to the throne at 15, and soon launches the Jianyuan Reforms, which his grandmother Empress Dowager Dou defeats</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">139 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">He sends <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-qian">Zhang Qian</a> to seek the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuezhi">Yuezhi</a>, the start of Han exploration of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-regions">Western Regions</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">119 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mobei">Battle of Mobei</a> the Han generals <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wei-qing">Wei Qing</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huo-qubing">Huo Qubing</a> force the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chanyu">chanyu</a> north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gobi-desert">Gobi Desert</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">111 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Han forces take Panyu and annex the whole of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanyue">Nanyue</a>, establishing ten <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commandery">commanderies</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">108 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Han takes over the lands of Gojoseon and establishes four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commandery">commanderies</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">91 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Crown Prince Ju's revolt is crushed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> after five days of fighting; he and his mother Empress Wei die</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">87 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Wu dies and the youngest son Liu Fuling succeeds as Emperor Zhao</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="early">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Early Life</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wen-of-han"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emperor-wen-of-han.jpg" alt="Emperor Wen of Han" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wen-of-han">Emperor Wen of Han</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:EmperorWenOfHan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Liu Che was the 11th son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-jing-of-han">Liu Qi</a>, the oldest living son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wen-of-han">Emperor Wen of Han</a>. His mother Wang Zhi had earlier been married to a commoner, Jin Wangsun, and had a daughter by him. Her mother Zang Er was told by a soothsayer that both of her daughters would one day be greatly honoured, and offered them to the crown prince Liu Qi, forcibly divorcing Wang Zhi from her husband. Liu Qi became Emperor Jing of Han on the death of Emperor Wen in 156 BCE, and on that day Wang Zhi gave birth to Liu Che. She claimed to have dreamed during her pregnancy that a sun fell into her womb. Liu Che was made Prince of Jiaodong on 16 May 153 BCE and was said to be his father's favourite son from a very young age.</p><p>The empress, Empress Bo, had no children, and Liu Rong, the son of Lady Li, was made crown prince in 153 BCE. Emperor Jing's sister Princess Guantao, whose offer to marry her daughter to Liu Rong Lady Li had rudely rejected, instead proposed the marriage of her daughter Chen Jiao to the five-year-old Liu Che. Consort Wang accepted. Princess Guantao then criticised Lady Li before the emperor, and in 150 BCE Emperor Jing deposed Liu Rong, who was arrested two years later and killed himself in custody. Wang was made empress, and Liu Che became crown prince in 149 BCE. In 141 BCE Emperor Jing died and Liu Che ascended the throne as Emperor Wu at the age of 15.</p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Family in the encyclopedia</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/encyclopedia/emperor-jing-of-han">Emperor Jing of Han<span>Father</span></a><a href="/encyclopedia/wei-zifu">Wei Zifu<span>Married</span></a></p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rise">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Rise</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Han court had until then followed a policy of non-interference, and kept the peace with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> by periodic marriage alliances (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heqin">heqin</a>). It had also left the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> states powerful enough to rebel in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rebellion-of-the-seven-states">Rebellion of the Seven States</a> under Emperor Jing. In late 141 BCE Wu took the advice of Confucian scholars and launched the Jianyuan Reforms: endorsing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucianism">Confucianism</a>, sending noblemen back to their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fiefs</a>, removing unsanctioned checkpoints, prosecuting criminal nobles and recruiting talented commoners. His grandmother, Grand Empress Dowager Dou, who held real power and supported the conservatives, defeated the reforms. His supporters Dou Ying and Tian Fen lost their positions, and his two mentors Wang Zang and Zhao Wan were arrested and forced to commit suicide in prison. Wu then spent some years playing the part of a docile pleasure-seeker, while political enemies used the childlessness of Empress Chen Jiao to argue for replacing him with his cousin Liu An, King of Huainan.</p><p>Wu secretly recruited young supporters of ordinary background into middle-level posts, forming an "insider court" that reported directly to him and counterbalanced the "outsider court" of ministers. In 138 BCE he used an imperial decree to send a fleet to the aid of Dong'ou against Minyue without the tiger tally that his grandmother held. In the same year the concubine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wei-zifu">Wei Zifu</a> became pregnant with his first child. Grand Empress Dowager Dou died in 135 BCE, leaving him in full control.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="deeds">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What They Did</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhang-qian"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zhang-qian.jpg" alt="Zhang Qian" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/zhang-qian">Zhang Qian</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ZhangQianTravels.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PHG, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After 135 BCE Wu turned to expansion. The Battle of Mayi in 133 BCE, a failed plan to ambush a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> force of 30,000 with 300,000 Han soldiers, ended the policy of peace, and he changed the army from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a> and infantry to mobile cavalry. Between 127 and 119 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wei-qing">Wei Qing</a> and his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huo-qubing">Huo Qubing</a> drove the Xiongnu from the Ordos Desert and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qilian-mountains">Qilian Mountains</a>, opening the Northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a>, and 700,000 soldier-settlers were sent to colonise the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu-corridor">Hexi Corridor</a>. In 139 BCE he had sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-qian">Zhang Qian</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuezhi">Yuezhi</a>; Zhang returned in 126 BCE and regular embassies to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-regions">Western Regions</a> followed. Han took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanyue">Nanyue</a> in 111 BCE, Dian in 109 BCE and Gojoseon in 108 BCE. In 102 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-guangli">Li Guangli</a>'s second army forced Dayuan to surrender 3,000 of its horses after a first army had been defeated in 103 BCE.</p><p>At home he founded the Imperial University in 136 BCE and, in 106 BCE, divided the empire into 13 prefectures with a supervisor for each. The Taichu calendar of 104 BCE began the year with the first month. To pay for his spending, his agricultural minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sang-hongyang">Sang Hongyang</a> devised state monopolies on salt and iron. He also introduced the practice of changing reign names, and worshipped the god Tai Yi and held the Feng and Shan sacrifices at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-tai">Mount Tai</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="opponents">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Opponents &amp; Critics</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/records-of-the-grand-historian"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/records-of-the-grand-historian.jpg" alt="Records of the Grand Historian" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/records-of-the-grand-historian">Records of the Grand Historian</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%8F%B2%E8%AE%B0.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Zhonghua Book Company, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Wu trusted officials who were harsh in punishment. One of them, Yi Zong, executed 200 prisoners who had committed no capital crime, and then their visiting friends. In 122 BCE Liu An and his brother Liu Ci were accused of plotting treason and killed themselves, and their families and many alleged conspirators were executed. In 117 BCE the minister Yan Yi, who had opposed a plan to extort double tribute from princes and marquesses, was executed for "internal defamation". When Li Ling surrendered to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> in 99 BCE, Wu had his clan executed. Wu is said to have borne a grudge against the historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sima-qian">Sima Qian</a>, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/records-of-the-grand-historian">Shiji</a> was less flattering to him than he wished, and to have had him castrated.</p><p>The wars strained the people. After Mobei the Han lost almost 80% of their warhorses, new levies burdened the peasants, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> showed a drop in population from famine and flight from taxes. About 100 BCE there were many peasant revolts, and an edict making officials answerable with their lives for unsuppressed revolts led them to hide the revolts. Wu's tours and search for immortality strained the treasury; twice a governor killed himself after failing to supply his whole train. The historian Michael Loewe called the reign the high point of "Modernist" policies. Wu is often compared to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-shi-huang">Qin Shi Huang</a>, and historians have treated him with ambivalence. He incarcerated nearly two hundred thousand people in special prisons; of his twelve prime ministers, three were executed and two killed themselves in office.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="last">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Last Years</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 96 BCE a series of witchcraft persecutions began. Wu, disturbed by a nightmare of being whipped by tiny puppets and by a sighting of an assassin who left no trace, ordered extensive investigations. Many accused, often high officials, were executed with their clans, beginning with the prime minister Gongsun He. The persecutions became tangled in a struggle over the succession. Crown Prince Ju disagreed with his father over policy, favouring leniency, and the official Jiang Chong and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuch</a> Su Wen, who had accused him before, claimed to find witchcraft items in his palace; Ju knew he had been framed. In 91 BCE Ju killed Jiang. Wu, told falsely that Ju was staging a coup, ordered Prime Minister Liu Qumao to put it down. After five days of fighting in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>, Ju fled and hanged himself when surrounded, and his mother Empress <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wei-zifu">Wei Zifu</a> killed herself.</p><p>Wu later came to see that many witchcraft accusations were false, had Su Wen burned and issued a public apology, the Repenting Edict of Luntai; wars and expansion generally ceased. By 88 BCE he was seriously ill. He chose his youngest son Liu Fuling, then six, as heir, named Huo Guang regent and ordered the execution of the boy's mother, Consort Zhao, for fear that she would become an uncontrollable empress dowager. He died in 87 BCE. Fuling succeeded as Emperor Zhao and Wu was buried at Maoling.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/dong-zhongshu"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dong-zhongshu.jpg" alt="Dong Zhongshu" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/dong-zhongshu">Dong Zhongshu</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:歷代聖賢半身像_冊_董仲舒_(Dong_Zhongshu).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Wu roughly doubled the size of the Han empire, and much of the territory he annexed is now part of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. At its height its borders ran from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fergana-valley">Fergana Valley</a> in the west to northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> in the east and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> in the south. Under him the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> opened trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, and contacts with western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eurasia">Eurasia</a> greatly increased. Historians have treated him with ambivalence: he officially promoted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucianism">Confucianism</a>, yet like <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-shi-huang">Qin Shi Huang</a> personally governed by a Legalist system of rewards and punishments. He is said to have been extravagant and superstitious, and to have killed thousands of people and their families in the Liu An affair, the Hengshan case, the witchcraft prosecutions and Prince Ju's revolt.</p><p>His reforms strengthened the emperor's power at the expense of the prime minister, and the post of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-documents">Shangshu</a>, once a keeper of documents, became the emperor's close adviser, which it remained until the end of the imperial era. An examination of over 100 young scholars in 140 BCE, in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dong-zhongshu">Dong Zhongshu</a> argued for Confucianism, marks the start of its establishment as imperial doctrine, though it is unclear how far Wu himself decided this, since the prime minister Wei Wan was also a Confucian. His Imperial Music Bureau gave its name to the yuefu style of poetry, and the fu style took shape at his court around Sima Xiangru. Maoling was later looted by the Chimei.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Emperor Wu of Han (156-87 BCE) ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> for 54 years from 141 BCE. He drove back the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a>, annexed lands from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> and promoted Confucian learning, but his wars, taxes and witchcraft persecutions brought heavy suffering, including the death of his own crown prince.</em></p>

